<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Titus (saint)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saint Titus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> was an early Christian missionary and companion of the apostle Paul, to whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> Letter to Titus is addressed. According to tradition he became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and he is the island's patron saint. His relic, his skull, is kept in the Church of Saint Titus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titus-saint.jpg" alt="Titus (saint)" width="300" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_Saint_Titos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
